            "Soul Shred": {
              "name": "灵魂撕裂 Soul Shred",
              "description": "<p>被孤寂杀死的生物很难被复活或被制作成不死。任何试图复活或唤起它所杀死的任意生物的行为，孤寂都会自动尝试反制（反制调整值 [[/r 1d20+13 #Counteract]]{+13}，反制位阶 3）。如果杀死该生物的孤寂本身也被杀死，则该生物可以正常从死亡中被带回或成为不死。</p>\n<p>Creatures killed by a guhdggi are difficult to bring back to life or animate as undead. The guhdggi automatically attempts to counteract any attempt to resurrect or animate any creature that it has killed (counteract modifier [[/r 1d20+13 #Counteract]]{+13}, counteract rank 3). If the guhdggi who slew the creature is itself slain, the creature can be brought back from death or become undead normally.</p>"
            },
            "Belch Butterflies": {
              "name": "喷吐蝴蝶 Belch Butterflies",
              "description": "<p>孤寂张开它的两张嘴，从其最深处的人形口中喷吐出一阵红色蝴蝶的爆发，形成一个@Template[cone|distance:15]。区域内所有生物必须尝试一次@Check[reflex|dc:22|options:area-effect,damaging-effect,inflicts:sickened]豁免，因为这些骇人的昆虫用它们锋利如剃刀的翅膀与超小型的爪子劈砍撕裂暴露的血肉。孤寂在[[/gmr 1d4 #Recharge Belch Butterflies]]{1d4轮}内无法再次使用喷吐蝴蝶。</p><hr /><p><strong>大成功</strong> 该生物不受蝴蝶造成的伤害。</p>\n<p><strong>成功</strong> 该生物受到来自蝴蝶的@Damage[2d6[slashing]|options:area-damage]伤害。</p>\n<p><strong>失败</strong> 该生物受到@Damage[4d6[slashing],1d6[bleed]|options:area-damage]{4d6挥砍伤害，1d6持续流血伤害}，并陷入@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.fesd1n5eVhpCSS18]{恶心 1}。</p>\n<p><strong>大失败</strong> 该生物受到@Damage[8d6[slashing],2d6[bleed]|options:area-damage]{8d6挥砍伤害，2d6持续流血伤害}，并陷入@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.fesd1n5eVhpCSS18]{恶心 2}。</p>\n<p>The guhdggi opens both of its mouths and, from its innermost humanoid mouth, belches forth a blast of red butterflies in a @Template[cone|distance:15]. All creatures in the area must attempt a @Check[reflex|dc:22|options:area-effect,damaging-effect,inflicts:sickened] save as the hideous insects' razor sharp wings and tiny claws slash and cut away at exposed flesh. The guhdggi can't Belch Butterflies again for [[/gmr 1d4 #Recharge Belch Butterflies]]{1d4 rounds}.</p><hr /><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature takes no damage from the butterflies.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes @Damage[2d6[slashing]|options:area-damage] damage from the butterflies.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes @Damage[4d6[slashing],1d6[bleed]|options:area-damage]{4d6 slashing damage, 1d6 persistent bleed damage}, and is @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.fesd1n5eVhpCSS18]{Sickened 1}.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes @Damage[8d6[slashing],2d6[bleed]|options:area-damage]{8d6 slashing damage, 2d6 persistent bleed damage}, and is @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.fesd1n5eVhpCSS18]{Sickened 2}.</p>"
            },
            "Cage Prey": {
              "name": "笼中猎物 Cage Prey",
              "description": "<p><strong>需求</strong> 孤寂以其发鞭擒拿了一个中型或更小的生物，且没有将任何生物关在笼中</p><hr /><p><strong>效果</strong> 孤寂尝试进行一次 @Check[athletics|against:reflex] 检定，对抗该 @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.kWc1fhmv9LBiTuei]{受擒} 生物的反射DC。若成功，它将该生物放入其背上携带的笼中。孤寂的发鞭不再擒拿该生物，并且它无法攻击其笼中的生物。</p>\n<p>被关笼的生物处于受擒状态。它在首次被关入笼中时，以及在其被关笼期间每个自身回合结束时，受到 @Damage[(2d4+6)[mental]] 伤害。被关笼的受害者可以尝试以DC 22检定来[[/act force-open dc=22]]或[[/act escape dc=22]]该笼子；或者它也可以攻击笼子的柳条栅栏，但只能使用徒手攻击或负载为轻或更低的武器。其他生物也可以攻击柳条栅栏，但若孤寂能够抵抗，则它们不能尝试破拆笼子，也不能协助被关笼生物的逃脱。对笼子的攻击以孤寂的AC结算；若笼子在一次攻击或法术中受到至少16点钝击或挥砍伤害，则被关笼的生物获得自由。</p>\n<p>如果孤寂死亡，它的笼子会崩塌成碎片。</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The guhdggi has grappled a Medium or smaller creature with its hair lash and doesn't have a creature caged</p><hr /><p><strong>Effect</strong> The guhdggi attempts an @Check[athletics|against:reflex] check opposed by the @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.conditionitems.Item.kWc1fhmv9LBiTuei]{Grabbed} creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it places the creature into the cage it carries on its back. The guhdggi's hair lash no longer grapples the creature, and it can't attack a creature in its cage.</p>\n<p>The caged creature is grabbed. It takes @Damage[(2d4+6)[mental]] damage when first caged and at the end of each of its turns while it's caged. A caged victim can attempt to [[/act force-open dc=22]] or [[/act escape dc=22]] the cage with a DC 22 check, or it can attack the cage's wicker bars, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. Other creatures can attack the wicker bars as well but can't attempt to Force Open the cage or aid a caged creature's Escape if the guhdggi can resist. Attacks against the cage resolve against the guhdggi's AC, and if the cage takes at least 16 bludgeoning or slashing damage from a single attack or spell, the caged creature gets free.</p>\n<p>If the guhdggi dies, its cage crumbles to fragments.</p>"
            },
            "Lurching Skewer": {
              "name": "突刺 Lurching Skewer",
              "description": "<p>孤寂先行走，然后用其持用的两把矛各进行一次打击，共进行两次矛打击。两次打击必须以同一个目标为对象。对每次打击照常应用孤寂的多重攻击减值。</p>\n<p>The guhdggi Strides, then makes two spear Strikes, one with each of the two spears it wields. Both Strikes must have the same target. Apply the guhdggi's multiple attack penalty to each Strike normally.</p>"
            },
            "Manifest Tool": {
              "name": "具现工具 Manifest Tool",
              "description": "<p><strong>频率</strong> 每小时一次</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>效果</strong> 孤寂从思绪中具现出一件工具。如果它的下一个动作是尝试进行一次可能受其所选工具影响的技能检定，则它在该技能检定上获得+2物品加值。随后该工具被摧毁。</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The guhdggi manifests a tool from its thoughts. If its next action is to attempt a skill check that could be affected by the tool it chose, it gains a +2 item bonus to the skill check. The tool is then destroyed.</p>"
            },
            "Ruin Wielder": {
              "name": "持骸者 Ruin Wielder",
              "description": "<p>当孤寂挥舞一把破损的非魔法武器时，他们不会承受武器处于破损状态的任何劣势。当孤寂挥舞它时，该武器变为一把+1强击武器，并在成功命中时额外造成1d6心灵伤害。</p>\n<p>When a guhdggi wields a broken non-magical weapon, they do not take any of the disadvantages of the weapon being broken. While the guhdggi wields it, the weapon becomes a +1 striking weapon that deals an additional 1d6 mental damage on a successful hit.</p>"
            },
            "Grab": {
              "name": "抓取 Grab",
              "description": "<p>@Localize[PF2E.NPC.Abilities.Glossary.Grab]</p>"
            }
          },
          "publicNotes": "<p>咕德吉（guhdggi）由那些在多次人生中踏过同一条朝圣路线之人的转世的灵魂，在遭到腐化后形成。这种灵堕魔看起来像一只臃肿的青蛙，前足的位置却长着修长纤细的人类手臂。它的眼睛鼓胀、似乎失明，却仍具备视力。咕德吉总是背着一个绑在背上的巨大柳条笼；同时，数十条绳索与绑带将一大堆破碎的武器、工具与杂物固定在它的腹部与两肋。当它行走时，会用前臂夹持的两根长矛作为支撑，踉跄跳跃，步态笨拙而难看。咕德吉最令人不安的特征，是它的第二张脸：当那张蛙口张开时，会露出其咽喉处的一张人脸，那张脸从一团纠结的蝴蝶巢中窥视出来。这张脸有着又长又黑、仿佛自有生命般舞动的头发，能够抽打出令人痛苦的鞭痕；当蛙嘴合拢时，那些发丝的末端会从蛙口的嘴角垂挂下来。</p>\n<p>尽管咕德吉已无法像大多数类人生物那样迅捷移动，它们仍然喜爱旅行，同时对那些能更轻松赶路的存在怀有凶暴的嫉妒。不过，也别小看咕德吉那跌撞踉跄的步伐！这恶魔尤其喜欢在朝圣路线偏僻的路段埋伏，突袭旅人，将受害者囚入背笼中，并吞噬俘虏的心智与他们曾到访之地的记忆。</p>\n<p>咕德吉体现了重复旅程的循环，以及“每一段旅程的终点即是下一段的起点”的概念。该概念的核心在于：没有任何旅程真正开始或结束——它们只是从上一段旅程中生发出来。</p>\n<hr />\n<p>当一个曾多次转世的邪恶凡人灵魂被从灵魂循环中强行扯出并遭到腐化时，它可能会经历一种可怖的蜕变，化为一种名为“灵堕魔”的恶魔。作为熵与断绝循环的力量，灵堕魔是丑恶的生物，其身体常呈现扭曲畸变；它们挥舞已被打碎却仍能运作的武器，而它们真正的面容也往往隐藏不见。它们展现出人们可能在不死生物身上看到的特征，例如腐烂的血肉或裸露的骨骼，然而灵堕魔确实仍然活着。</p>\n<p>每一只灵堕魔都象征着某一特定循环的破碎，无论那循环来自自然界还是社会传统。对灵堕魔而言，没有什么比吞食一个多次转世的生物更“美味”；而那些理论上即将升至真正觉悟者，则是最可口的一餐。被灵堕魔杀死者也会被从循环中撕裂——幸运的人会前往白骨庭接受审判，但更多的人则会被腐化，化为更多这类恐怖的恶魔。</p>\n<p>善解的追随者认为灵堕魔尤为可憎，并将其视为能想象到的最巨大威胁之一。灵堕魔最常见于转世循环最为强盛的世界区域；与罗刹与鬼（oni）一样，它们与物质位面的关联最为紧密。</p>\n<h2>转世的灵魂</h2>\n<p>在某些情况下，你会知道灵堕魔袭击的生物是否拥有转世的灵魂。诸如轮回者（samsaran）等族裔永远是转世的灵魂，而PC的背景故事，甚至他们的背景，也常能表明其前世的存在。《鬼季》中的所有PC都符合转世的灵魂的条件，因为他们在数十年间一遍又一遍地重历人生。若你需要为裁定灵堕魔的能力而判定某个生物的灵魂是否为转世的灵魂，进行一次@Check[flat|dc:11]——若成功，则其拥有转世的灵魂。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔半神</h2>\n<p>最强大的灵堕魔是半神，尽管它们的教团相当罕见。灵堕魔半神在死亡后往往会再度转世；像库加普提（Kugaptee）这样的灵堕魔半神，更恰当的称呼是“灵堕魔苦行者”。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔叛徒</h2>\n<p>尽管大多数灵堕魔的本性纯粹为混乱邪恶，传说中仍有极其罕见的灵堕魔得以挣脱自身本性并完成超升，在死亡后转世为神使（kami）或其他更为仁善的灵体，并在此过程中褪去灵堕魔特征。当然，这些“叛徒”会被留下的灵堕魔视为最美味的佳肴。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔蝴蝶</h2>\n<p>各种类型的灵堕魔共有的最离奇特征之一，是那些似乎跟随它们盘旋的血红色蝴蝶——它们会在灵堕魔身上筑巢，或周期性地从其伤口中涌出。这些令人不安的昆虫是灵堕魔思绪的具现，象征着它们曾经经历却又背弃的无数前世。一旦灵堕魔蝴蝶飞离其源头超过几尺，便不会存在太久，而会化作烟雾消散在空气中。</p>\n<h2>其他灵堕魔</h2>\n<p>除本页呈现的四种之外，还有许多其他类型的灵堕魔。其他例子包括：复制尸体的恪尸魔（哭泣灵堕魔）、被称为痂苦魔（潜伏灵堕魔）的社会渗透者、亵渎死者的煞星魔（畸形灵堕魔），以及强大而残暴的稽折魔（无头灵堕魔）。</p>\n<p>A guhdggi forms from the corrupted reincarnated souls of those who have trod the same pilgrimage route through multiple lives. This type of nindoru looks like a bloated frog with long, slender human arms instead of front feet. Its eyes appear bulbous and blind yet are capable of sight. A guhdggi always carries a large wicker cage strapped to their back, while dozens of cords and straps affix a trove of broken weapons, tools, and items to their belly and flanks. When they walk, they do so with the aid of two long spears clutched in their forearms, stumbling and hopping in an ungainly gait. The most unsettling feature of the guhdggi is its second face, revealed when the frog-like mouth opens to reveal a human face peering from its throat amid a tangled nest of butterflies. This face has long, dark, animated hair capable of inflicting painful lashes, the lengths of which hang from the corner of the frog's mouth when its lips are closed.</p>\n<p>While the guhdggi is no longer capable of movement as swift as most humanoids walk, they still enjoy traveling while harboring a ferocious jealousy of those who can do so with greater ease. One would do well not to underestimate the stumbling stagger of a guhdggi, though! The fiend particularly delights in lying in wait along isolated sections of pilgrimage routes to ambush travelers, trapping victims in their cages and feeding upon their captive's mind and memories of previously visited places.</p>\n<p>Guhdggis embody the cycles of repeated journeys and the concept that every journey taken ends at the start of the next. Central to this concept is the notion that no journey ever truly begins or ends—they just emerge from the previous one.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>When an evil mortal soul that has been reincarnated multiple times is wrenched from the cycle of souls and corrupted, it can undergo a horrific transformation into a fiend known as a nindoru. A force for entropy and the breaking of cycles, nindorus are hideous creatures whose bodies often appear distorted, who wield weapons that have been shattered yet still function, and whose true face is often hidden from view. They display traits one might expect to see in an undead creature, such as decaying flesh or exposed bones, yet a nindoru is very much still alive.</p>\n<p>Each nindoru epitomizes the fracture of a specific cycle, be it one from the natural world or from societal traditions. To the nindoru, nothing is more delicious than the consumption of a creature who has reincarnated multiple times, with those who are theoretically about to ascend to true enlightenment presenting the tastiest meal. Those slain by a nindoru are themselves torn from the cycle—the lucky ones move on to the Boneyard for judgment, but many are instead corrupted to form more of these horrific fiends.</p>\n<p>Followers of Sangpotshi find nindoru to be particularly abhorrent and count them among the greatest threats imaginable. Most commonly found in regions of the world where the reincarnation cycle is strongest, nindorus are, like rakshasas and oni, associated most strongly with the Material Plane.</p>\n<h2>Reincarnated Souls</h2>\n<p>In some cases, you'll know if a creature a nindoru attacks has a reincarnated soul. Ancestries like samsarans are always reincarnated souls, and a PC's backstory or even their background can often indicate the presence of a previous life. All of the PCs in Season of Ghosts qualify as reincarnated souls, as they've relived their lives over and over for decades. If you ever need to determine if a creature's soul is reincarnated for the purpose of adjudicating a nindoru's ability, attempt a @Check[flat|dc:11]—on a success, it has a reincarnated soul.</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Demigods</h2>\n<p>The most powerful nindorus are demigods, although their cults are quite rare. Prone to reincarnation after death, nindoru demigods like Kugaptee are more properly known as \"nindoru ascetics.\"</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Traitors</h2>\n<p>While most nindorus are purely chaotic evil in nature, legends tell of exceptionally rare nindorus who manage to escape their own nature and ascend, reincarnating after death into kami or other more benevolent spirits and shedding the nindoru trait as they do so. Of course, these \"traitors\" are regarded as the most delicious meals imaginable by the nindorus left behind.</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Butterflies</h2>\n<p>One of the strangest shared traits of the various types of nindoru are the blood-red butterflies that seem to follow them around, nest on their bodies, or periodically emerge from their wounds. These unnerving insects are physical manifestations of the nindorus' thoughts and symbolize the countless past lives they once lived but turned their backs on. Nindoru butterflies never last long once they flutter more than a few feet from their source, fading away into smoke that quickly dissipates into the air.</p>\n<h2>Other Nindorus</h2>\n<p>Many other types of nindorus exist beyond the four presented on these pages. Others include the corpse-copying akashtis (sobbing nindorus), infiltrators of society known as kagekumas (lurking nindorus), the dead defiling shisagishins (crooked nindorus), and the powerful and brutal argyrzeis (headless nindorus).</p>",
          "prototypeToken": "孤寂 Guhdggi"
        },
