            "Manifest Bone Club": {
              "name": "具现骨制棍棒 Manifest Bone Club",
              "description": "<p><strong>需求</strong> 莫啖魔没有携带骨制木棒</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>效果</strong> 莫啖魔把手伸进喉咙深入胃里，拉拽出一根人类股骨，它可以将其当作骨制木棒来挥舞。莫啖魔对一个生物进行打击后，骨制木棒会粉碎，但其锋利的碎片仍大致保持原来的形状；用粉碎的骨制木棒进行的打击会造成挥砍和流血伤害。莫啖魔用粉碎的骨制木棒进行打击后，或若莫啖魔丢下骨制木棒，木棒便会被摧毁，而灵堕魔可以显现一根新的骨制木棒。</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The meokdan isn't carrying a bone club</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The meokdan reaches down its throat into its stomach and pulls out a human femur that it can wield as a bone club. After the meokdan Strikes a creature, the bone club shatters, but its sharp fragments remain in roughly the same shape; Strikes with a shattered bone club inflict slashing and bleed damage. After the meokdan Strikes with a shattered bone club, or if the meokdan drops the bone club, the club is destroyed, and the nindoru can Manifest a new bone club.</p>"
            },
            "Quick Snack": {
              "name": "快速啃食 Quick Snack",
              "description": "<p><strong>触发</strong> 莫啖魔用其啃咬对一个活体生物造成重击</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>效果</strong> 莫啖魔对该生物进行啃食。</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The meokdan critically hits a living creature with its bite</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The meokdan Snacks on the creature.</p>"
            },
            "Snack": {
              "name": "啃食 Snack",
              "description": "<p>莫啖魔尝试对相邻的生物进行一次啃咬打击，或咀嚼其所携带的手持物体。若该生物为活体或该物体为食物，则莫啖魔欢喜地咯咯作响并恢复 @Damage[(1d8+8)[healing]] 生命值。若该生物为不死或构装体，或该物体不可食用，则莫啖魔挫败地尖叫，并在其下个回合结束前，其攻击掷骰与豁免骰获得+1状态加值。莫啖魔在1轮内无法再次啃食。</p>\n<p>@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.bestiary-effects.Item.d0aJSi7GI6AhBlAJ]{效果：啃食}</p>\n<p>The meokdan attempts a bite Strike against an adjacent creature or chews on a handheld object they're carrying. If the creature is living or the object is food, the meokdan gurgles in delight and restores @Damage[(1d8+8)[healing]] Hit Points. If the creature is undead or a construct, or the object is inedible, the meokdan shrieks in frustration and gains a +1 status bonus to attack rolls and saving throws until the end of its next turn. The meokdan can't Snack again for 1 round.</p>\n<p>@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.bestiary-effects.Item.d0aJSi7GI6AhBlAJ]{Effect: Snack}</p>"
            }
          },
          "publicNotes": "<p>作为其同类中最弱小的一支，meokdan 承受着持续而痛苦的饥饿，因为它们缺乏更强大的灵堕魔从转世的灵魂中汲取养分的能力。这种饥饿驱使 meokdan 不断觅食，并常常迫使它们吞吃泥土、石头和垃圾——这些东西几乎（如果有的话）没有任何营养价值。无论吃下多少，meokdan 的体态始终瘦得令人痛苦。无论饥饿到什么程度，它们也从不会饿死。</p>\n<p>meokdan 消瘦的骨架外形近似人类，浓密纠结的毛发不仅从头顶生长，还直接从脸上蔓生，遮蔽一切，只留下它们张开的巨口清晰可见。它们的手臂看似折断，异常细长，手指同样修长，末端是参差破裂的指甲。它们的皮肤上布满细小的开放性溃疡。</p>\n<p>meokdan 正是“食物循环”的化身：从吞食开始，到最终自己也会被吞食——无论是被掠食者啃噬，还是被环境从其残骸中滋长而出。</p>\n<hr />\n<p>当一个曾多次转世的邪恶凡人灵魂被硬生生从轮回之环中扯出并遭到腐化时，它可能会经历可怖的蜕变，成为一种名为灵堕魔的恶魔。作为熵增与破坏循环的力量，灵堕魔是丑陋的怪物，其躯体常呈扭曲畸变之态；它们挥舞已被打碎却仍能运作的武器，而它们真正的面容也常被遮蔽难见。它们显露出人们会在不死生物身上看到的特征，例如腐烂的血肉或外露的骨骼，然而灵堕魔确实仍是活物。</p>\n<p>每一只灵堕魔都体现着某一种特定循环的断裂——无论那循环源自自然界还是社会传统。对灵堕魔而言，没有什么比吞食一个经历多次转世的生物更美味；而那些理论上即将迈向真正觉悟者，更是最可口的一餐。被灵堕魔杀死者同样会被撕裂出轮回：幸运者会前往骨冢接受审判，但更多的人则会被腐化，化作更多这种恐怖的恶魔。</p>\n<p>善解的追随者认为灵堕魔尤为可憎，并将其列为最可怕的威胁之一。灵堕魔最常出没于转世轮回最为强盛的世界区域；并且和罗刹与鬼族一样，它们与物质位面的关联最为密切。</p>\n<h2>转世的灵魂</h2>\n<p>在某些情况下，你会知道灵堕魔所攻击的生物是否拥有转世的灵魂。像轮回者这样的族裔总是转世的灵魂，而 PC 的身世背景甚至其背景也常能表明其前世的存在。《鬼季》中的所有 PC 都符合转世的灵魂的条件，因为他们在数十年间一遍又一遍地重活自己的人生。若你需要为了裁定灵堕魔的能力而判定某个生物的灵魂是否为转世灵魂，进行一次 @Check[flat|dc:11]——成功则其拥有转世的灵魂。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔半神</h2>\n<p>最强大的灵堕魔是半神，尽管崇拜它们的教团相当罕见。灵堕魔半神如库加普提（Kugaptee）死后往往会再度转世，更恰当的称呼是“灵堕魔苦行者”。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔叛徒</h2>\n<p>尽管大多数灵堕魔本质上都是纯粹的混乱邪恶，传说中却有极其罕见的灵堕魔设法挣脱自身天性并得以超升，死后转世为神使或其他更仁善的灵体，并在此过程中褪去灵堕魔特征。当然，这些“叛徒”也被那些仍留下的灵堕魔视为最不可思议的美餐。</p>\n<h2>灵堕魔蝴蝶</h2>\n<p>各种类型的灵堕魔之间，共享特征中最离奇之一，是那群仿佛会围着它们盘旋的血红色蝴蝶：它们在其体表筑巢，或周期性地从其伤口中涌出。这些令人不安的昆虫是灵堕魔思想的实体化显现，象征着它们曾经历的无数前世，却已选择背弃。灵堕魔蝴蝶一旦离开其源头飞出数尺之外，便不会存在太久，会化作烟雾并迅速消散在空气中。</p>\n<h2>其他灵堕魔</h2>\n<p>除本页展示的四种外，还存在许多其他类型的灵堕魔。其中包括：复制尸体的恪尸魔（哭泣灵堕魔）；被称为痂苦魔（潜伏灵堕魔）的社会渗透者；亵渎死者的煞星魔（畸形灵堕魔）；以及强大而残暴的稽折魔（无头灵堕魔）。</p>\n<p>As the least of their kind, meokdans suffer a constant and painful hunger, for they lack the ability of more powerful nindorus to derive nourishment from a reincarnated soul. This hunger drives the meokdan to constantly seek food and often compels them to eat dirt, stones, and refuse that offer little (if any) nutritional value. Regardless of how much they eat, a meokdan always remains painfully thin in stature. Regardless of how hungry they get, they never starve to death.</p>\n<p>A meokdan's emaciated frame appears human in shape, with thick, tangled hair that grows directly from their face as well as the rest of their head, leaving only the meokdan's gaping mouth visible. Their arms appear to be broken and unnaturally long, with equally long fingers tipped with ragged nails. Small, open sores pockmark their skin.</p>\n<p>Meokdans epitomize the cycle of food, from consumption to the creature themselves being consumed in the end, be it by predators or the environment growing from their remains.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>When an evil mortal soul that has been reincarnated multiple times is wrenched from the cycle of souls and corrupted, it can undergo a horrific transformation into a fiend known as a nindoru. A force for entropy and the breaking of cycles, nindorus are hideous creatures whose bodies often appear distorted, who wield weapons that have been shattered yet still function, and whose true face is often hidden from view. They display traits one might expect to see in an undead creature, such as decaying flesh or exposed bones, yet a nindoru is very much still alive.</p>\n<p>Each nindoru epitomizes the fracture of a specific cycle, be it one from the natural world or from societal traditions. To the nindoru, nothing is more delicious than the consumption of a creature who has reincarnated multiple times, with those who are theoretically about to ascend to true enlightenment presenting the tastiest meal. Those slain by a nindoru are themselves torn from the cycle—the lucky ones move on to the Boneyard for judgment, but many are instead corrupted to form more of these horrific fiends.</p>\n<p>Followers of Sangpotshi find nindoru to be particularly abhorrent and count them among the greatest threats imaginable. Most commonly found in regions of the world where the reincarnation cycle is strongest, nindorus are, like rakshasas and oni, associated most strongly with the Material Plane.</p>\n<h2>Reincarnated Souls</h2>\n<p>In some cases, you'll know if a creature a nindoru attacks has a reincarnated soul. Ancestries like samsarans are always reincarnated souls, and a PC's backstory or even their background can often indicate the presence of a previous life. All of the PCs in Season of Ghosts qualify as reincarnated souls, as they've relived their lives over and over for decades. If you ever need to determine if a creature's soul is reincarnated for the purpose of adjudicating a nindoru's ability, attempt a @Check[flat|dc:11]—on a success, it has a reincarnated soul.</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Demigods</h2>\n<p>The most powerful nindorus are demigods, although their cults are quite rare. Prone to reincarnation after death, nindoru demigods like Kugaptee are more properly known as \"nindoru ascetics.\"</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Traitors</h2>\n<p>While most nindorus are purely chaotic evil in nature, legends tell of exceptionally rare nindorus who manage to escape their own nature and ascend, reincarnating after death into kami or other more benevolent spirits and shedding the nindoru trait as they do so. Of course, these \"traitors\" are regarded as the most delicious meals imaginable by the nindorus left behind.</p>\n<h2>Nindoru Butterflies</h2>\n<p>One of the strangest shared traits of the various types of nindoru are the blood-red butterflies that seem to follow them around, nest on their bodies, or periodically emerge from their wounds. These unnerving insects are physical manifestations of the nindorus' thoughts and symbolize the countless past lives they once lived but turned their backs on. Nindoru butterflies never last long once they flutter more than a few feet from their source, fading away into smoke that quickly dissipates into the air.</p>\n<h2>Other Nindorus</h2>\n<p>Many other types of nindorus exist beyond the four presented on these pages. Others include the corpse-copying akashtis (sobbing nindorus), infiltrators of society known as kagekumas (lurking nindorus), the dead defiling shisagishins (crooked nindorus), and the powerful and brutal argyrzeis (headless nindorus).</p>",
          "prototypeToken": "莫啖魔 Meokdan"
        },
