          "pages": {
            "Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Ric": {
              "name": "花生馅糯米黑芝麻汤 Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Ric",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\">黑芝麻糊配花生馅汤圆</h2>\n<img class=\"float-right\" src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-seasamesoup.webp\" alt=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\" title=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-seasamesoup.webp')\" />\n<p class=\"no-indent\">这些营养丰富、带有坚果香气的汤品，黑得如同神门昏暗无光的天空，带着烘烤后的微苦回甘，常在夜晚作为甜点食用，以强健身体、抵御夜间寒冷潮湿的天气。人们有时会加入糯米团子，常以花生碎为馅；这些团子带来额外的甜蜜惊喜，也提醒食客：即使在最黑暗的夜里，也可能藏匿着喜悦。</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>原料</h2>\n  <p>2杯烤黑芝麻种子</p>\n  <p>1/2杯茉莉香米</p>\n  <p>3 1/2块冰糖</p>\n  <p>1/2汤匙盐</p>\n  <p>2汤匙烤芝麻油</p>\n  <p>12杯水</p>\n  <p>1碗糯米粉</p>\n  <p>1碗烘烤过的花生碎</p>\n  <p>1/4杯糖</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>将烤芝麻种子磨成细粉。</li>\n    <li>将烤芝麻粉、茉莉香米、冰糖、盐和烤芝麻油放入锅中。</li>\n    <li>向锅中加入6杯水。</li>\n    <li>加热至微沸后转小火炖煮，因为缓慢熬煮能让汤更顺滑。</li>\n    <li>接下来的一到两个小时里，定期搅拌以使汤的质地均匀；让米粒完全煮熟并融入汤中。按需要加水，并注意别让汤糊锅。</li>\n    <li>将糯米粉加少许水混合，揉捏成面团。若面团变得过稀，可再加些水以保持弹性；注意别让外皮破裂。</li>\n    <li>将面团捏成面皮，每张约1 1/2英寸（3.8厘米）宽。</li>\n    <li>将糖与烘烤过的花生碎混合制成馅料。</li>\n    <li>在每张面皮中包入馅料，捏紧封口搓成球。</li>\n    <li>另起一锅，加入汤圆和6杯水，煮至沸腾。小心搅拌，确保汤圆不会粘在锅壁或汤勺上。待汤圆浮起后将其捞出，放入芝麻糊中。</li>\n    <li>将芝麻糊与汤圆盛入小碗。趁温热食用。</li>\n  </ul>\n</section>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\">Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls</h2>\n<img class=\"float-right\" src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-seasamesoup.webp\" alt=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\" title=\"Black Sesame Soup with Peanut-filled Glutinous Rice Balls\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-seasamesoup.webp')\" />\n<p class=\"no-indent\">These nutritious, nutty soups, as black as Shenmen’s ill-lit skies, possess a roasted, bittersweet tinge and are often consumed as evening desserts to fortify the body against the night’s cold and damp weather. Glutinous rice balls, often filled with ground peanuts, are sometimes added; these rice balls provide an additional sweet surprise and remind diners of the possibility of hidden joys even on the darkest nights.</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>Ingredients</h2>\n  <p>2 cups roasted black sesame seeds</p>\n  <p>1/2 cup jasmine rice</p>\n  <p>3 1/2 chunks rock sugar</p>\n  <p>1/2 tablespoon salt</p>\n  <p>2 tablespoons roasted sesame oil</p>\n  <p>12 cups water</p>\n  <p>1 bowl glutinous rice flour</p>\n  <p>1 bowl toasted ground peanuts</p>\n  <p>1/4 cup sugar</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Ground the roasted sesame seeds into fine powder.</li>\n    <li>Place the roasted sesame powder, jasmine rice, rock sugar, salt, and roast sesame oil into a pot.</li>\n    <li>Add 6 cups of water into the pot.</li>\n    <li>Bring to a simmer and cook on low heat, as you want slow cooking for a smoother soup.</li>\n    <li>Over the next hour or two, stir regularly to even out the texture of the soup; let the rice grains be totally cooked and absorbed into the soup. Add water as needed, and be careful not to let the soup burn.</li>\n    <li>Mix the glutinous rice flour with a bit of water, and pinch into dough. If the dough becomes too thin, add more water to retain its elasticity; be careful not to let the skin break.</li>\n    <li>Pinch the dough into wrappers, each approximately 1 1/2 inches (3.8 cm) wide.</li>\n    <li>Mix the sugar and toasted ground peanuts to get the filling.</li>\n    <li>Stuff each wrapper with filling, and pinch to seal into balls.</li>\n    <li>In a separate pot, add the rice balls and 6 cups of water, and bring to a boil. Stir carefully to ensure the rice balls don’t stick to the sides of the pot or the ladle. Once the rice balls float to the top, remove them and deposit into the sesame soup.</li>\n    <li>Serve the soup and rice balls in small bowls. Eat when warm.</li>\n  </ul>\n</section>"
            },
            "Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes": {
              "name": "神门椒盐月饼 Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\">神门椒盐月饼</h2>\n<img class=\"float-right\" src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-mooncakes.webp\" alt=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\" title=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-mooncakes.webp')\" />\n<p class=\"no-indent\">这些薄而酥脆的烘焙点心在神门很受欢迎，用以纪念首长夜的丰收月。尽管更为讲究的食客可能会轻蔑地认为这些带胡椒味、形似饼干的圆片“不算正宗月饼”，但在物资匮乏的神门，各地村落都对这类层叠酥点中咸鲜、辛辣与柑橘风味的旋涡般交织赞不绝口。</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>原料</h2>\n  <p>1 汤匙白胡椒粉（可按口味增减）</p>\n  <p>1 汤匙玫瑰浸渍糖</p>\n  <p>1 1/2 茶匙盐（可按口味增减）</p>\n  <p>2 把捣碎的橘子皮</p>\n  <p>2 杯炸红葱头</p>\n  <p>1 杯芝麻种子</p>\n  <p>1 杯瓜子</p>\n  <p>1 份嬛族语-舒国酥皮面团（也称嬛族语-舒国水油面团），擀成 24×24 英寸的面片（约 60×60 厘米）</p>\n  <p>1 杯猪油</p>\n  <p>2 个蛋黄，打散</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>将烤箱预热至 350°F（180°C）。</li>\n    <li>制作馅料：将红葱头、橘子皮以及两种种子各取 1 杯混合，铺在烤盘上，烘烤至种子变黄。随后倒入碗中放凉。</li>\n    <li>将胡椒、糖、盐和剩余红葱头均匀拌入酥皮面团中并揉匀。若面团太干，加入猪油。</li>\n    <li>将揉好的面团分成 8 个 3×3 英寸（约 7.5×7.5 厘米）的面团球。</li>\n    <li>将每个面团球再混合揉一次。按需加入猪油，使质地更顺滑。</li>\n    <li>将由红葱头、橘子皮与种子组成的馅料等分，并将其中 1 份分配给每个面团球。</li>\n    <li>捏紧每个面团球封口，将其压扁成圆饼状，再次捏合使封口均匀。确保外皮尽可能薄，但不要让馅料露出。</li>\n    <li>将封好的酥点摆放在铺好烘焙纸的烤盘上，并在每个表面刷上打散的蛋黄。</li>\n    <li>将烤盘置于烤箱中层，烘烤约 20 分钟，或至金黄。</li>\n    <li>月饼配茶道一同享用，最好是发酵或陈年的茶。</li>\n  </ul>\n</section>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\">Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes</h2>\n<img class=\"float-right\" src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-mooncakes.webp\" alt=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\" title=\"Shenmen Salt and Pepper Mooncakes\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-mooncakes.webp')\" />\n<p class=\"no-indent\">These thin, flaky baked goods are popular in Shenmen for commemorating the harvest moon of First Long Night. While more patrician diners might scorn these peppery biscuit-like discs as not being “proper mooncakes,” villages across resource-scarce Shenmen swear by these layered pastries’ swirl of savory saltiness, peppery heat, and citrus notes.</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>Ingredients</h2>\n  <p>1 tablespoon white pepper powder (add or reduce to taste)</p>\n  <p>1 tablespoon rose-infused sugar</p>\n  <p>1 1/2 teaspoons salt (add or reduce to taste)</p>\n  <p>2 handfuls crushed tangerine peels</p>\n  <p>2 cups fried shallots</p>\n  <p>1 cup sesame seeds</p>\n  <p>1 cup melon seeds</p>\n  <p>1 portion Tian-Shu flaky pastry dough (also known as Tian-Shu water-oil dough), rolled out into a 24×24 inch sheet (approximately 60×60 cm)</p>\n  <p>1 cup lard</p>\n  <p>2 egg yolks, beaten</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Heat oven to 350°F (180°C).</li>\n    <li>Make the filling by mixing 1 cup each of shallots, peels, and both seeds onto a baking tray, and bake until the seeds turn yellow. Then pour them out into a bowl to cool.</li>\n    <li>Mix and knead the pepper, sugar, salt, and remaining shallots evenly into the pastry dough. If the dough is too dry, add lard.</li>\n    <li>Separate the kneaded dough into 8 3×3 inch (approximately 7.5×7.5 cm) balls.</li>\n    <li>Mix and knead each ball again. Add lard as needed to smoothen the texture.</li>\n    <li>Separate the filling of shallots, peels, and seeds into equal portions, and allocate 1 equal portion to each ball of dough.</li>\n    <li>Pinch each ball to seal them, flatten them into discs, and pinch again to seal them evenly. Ensure the dough is as thin as possible without letting the filling stick out.</li>\n    <li>Arrange the sealed pastries on a lined baking sheet, and brush each with beaten egg.</li>\n    <li>Bake for about 20 minutes in the middle of the oven, or until golden brown.</li>\n    <li>Serve mooncakes with tea, preferably fermented or aged.</li>\n  </ul>\n</section>"
            },
            "Festival Foods": {
              "name": "节日食物 Festival Foods",
              "text": "<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Foods\">节日食物</h1>\n<p>以下两种点心在首长夜期间极受欢迎。</p>\n<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Foods\">Festival Foods</h1>\n<p>The following two treats are wildly popular during First Long Night.</p>"
            },
            "Festival Fashion": {
              "name": "节日风尚 Festival Fashion",
              "text": "<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Fashion\">节日风尚</h1>\n<p>尽管在首长夜期间穿着的确切风格与装束会因城镇而异，但在整个神门，各种固定要素始终延续。</p>\n<h2>轻装武备</h2>\n<p>带有魔法、能够提供持久照明的武器很受欢迎，它们既可充当光源，也能在需要时用于防身。尽管并非所有不死都对强光有如此极端的弱点，但它们全都能在黑暗中视物；而无需空出一只手去举火把或提灯笼，往往就是生与死的差别。</p>\n<h2>月饼与茶</h2>\n<p>社区会聚在一起分享这些点心——形状如同丰收满月——并配以浓烈、陈年的茶。更富裕的厨房会用奢华原料为月饼加馅，比如桂花浸香的莲蓉与咸蛋；而更朴素的储藏间则用盐、胡椒、炸葱头或捣碎的橘皮变出更简单的馅料。参加庆典的人通常会在腰带上挂一个小柳条篮，用来装多带的月饼。</p>\n<h2>红绳</h2>\n<p>把红绳系在手腕上，参加庆典的人是在致敬一项来自@UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog18sangpo]{善解}的根源：这种线绳会吸收幽灵（特征）的影响，并在抵御敌对魂魄之后自行脱落。若有一条掉了，别把它捡起来，除非你希望不死再次登门！相反，让它们被冬日风暴冲刷带走吧。</p>\n<img src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-first-long-night-fashion.webp\" data-tooltip=\"Festival Fashion\" alt=\"Festival go-er\" class=\"float-right\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-first-long-night-fashion.webp')\" />\n<h2>迷你灯笼</h2>\n<p>并非人人都买得起发光武器，即便买得起的人也常会佩戴一盏超小型灯笼蜡烛——按传统用一段短链挂在腰带或扣具上。在太阳升起、夜晚的最后几分钟点亮这盏私人灯笼，象征着首长夜的结束。</p>\n<h2>与蟋蟀共饮</h2>\n<p>村民常把蟋蟀喂给蜘蛛，希望借此劝阻告密者（以聒噪的蟋蟀象征），并象征性地满足络新妇的饥欲。秋季时，许多人会把装着酒精谷物糊的小碗留作献礼，献给这些蟋蟀的魂魄：既是乞求宽恕，也是感谢它们作为祭品替身所扮演的角色。参加庆典的人往往会带着几个小葫芦容器装酒精，以便需要时给空碗续上；或者带着装有蟋蟀的小柳条笼，好把它们喂给蜘蛛。</p>\n<h2>披挂森林</h2>\n<p>为护雨雪，参加庆典的人偏爱斗篷、斗笠，以及用上蜡的稻草或竹子制成的鞋。这些材料随处可得，天然防水且易于加工。它们泥褐色的色调，也能在神门满是怪物的荒野中提供出色而实惠的伪装。当地人把这种做法称为“披挂森林”。</p>\n<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Fashion\">Festival Fashion</h1>\n<p>While exact styles and outfits worn during First Long Night can vary from town to town, certain touchstones persist throughout Shenmen.</p>\n<h2>Lightly Armed</h2>\n<p>Weapons bearing magic that impart lasting illumination are popular for serving as light sources that can be used for defense as needed. While not all undead possess such extreme light vulnerability, they can all see in the dark, and not having to have a hand free to hold a torch or lantern can mean the difference between life and death.</p>\n<h2>Mooncakes and Tea</h2>\n<p>Communities gather to share these delicacies, shaped like the fullness of the harvest moon, pairing them alongside strong, aged tea. Wealthier kitchens stuff mooncakes with luxurious ingredients, such as osmanthus-infused lotus paste and salted eggs, while humbler pantries conjure simpler fare from salt, pepper, fried shallots, or crushed tangerine peel. A festival-goer typically travels with a small wicker basket on the belt to hold extra mooncakes.</p>\n<h2>Red Strings</h2>\n<p>By wearing red strings around the wrist, festival-goers honor a @UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog18sangpo]{Sangpotshi} tradition that asserts such threads absorb ghostly influence and fall off after resisting hostile spirits. If one falls, don’t pick them up unless you wish for return visits from the undead! Instead, leave them to be washed away by winter’s storms.</p>\n<img src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-first-long-night-fashion.webp\" data-tooltip=\"Festival Fashion\" alt=\"Festival go-er\" class=\"float-right\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-first-long-night-fashion.webp')\" />\n<h2>Miniature Lanterns</h2>\n<p>Not everyone can afford a glowing weapon, and even those who do often wear a single miniature lantern candle that’s traditionally hooked via a short chain to a belt or buckle. Lighting this personal lantern in the last minutes of the night as the sun rises symbolizes the end of First Long Night.</p>\n<h2>Drinking with the Crickets</h2>\n<p>Villagers regularly feed crickets to spiders, hoping to discourage tattletales (represented by the noisy crickets) and symbolically satiate the jorogumo’s hungers. In fall, many leave tiny bowls of alcoholic grain-mush as overtures to these crickets’ spirits, both to beg forgiveness as well as give thanks for their roles as sacrificial proxies. Festival-goers often carry a few small gourd containers to carry alcohol so they can refill empty bowls as needed or, alternatively, small wicker cages that contain crickets that can be fed to spiders.</p>\n<h2>Wearing the Forest</h2>\n<p>To ward off rain and snow, festival-goers favor capes, conical hats, and shoes of waxed straw or bamboo. These materials are ever-present, naturally water-repellent, and easy to work with. Their muddy browns also provide admirable yet affordable camouflage within Shenmen’s monster-filled wilderness. Locals refer to this practice as “wearing the forest.”</p>"
            },
            "Lantern Making": {
              "name": "制作天灯 Lantern Making",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Lantern Making\">制作天灯</h2>\n<p>天灯最初是在舒国时代为传递军事信号而发展出来的。如今这项古老的做法有了不同的文化意义：象征团聚，以及人们的愿望得以上达天穹。</p>\n<p>这些天灯以竹为骨架，用上过油的米纸制成。其装饰常采用鸟类（但绝不用乌鸦或渡鸦，因为著名的络新妇极其厌恶天狗）、巨龙以及其他飞行生物等奇巧造型。</p>\n<p>曾有一段时间，蜘蛛是天灯设计中极受欢迎的主题，或许是为了向络新妇统治者示好；然而这一风潮在数年前便失宠了。为讨朗洛依夫人欢心，官员们资助了一场盛大的制灯庆典，并委托制作一盏巨型天灯，其造型为金色的圆蛛，庞大到连丰收之月都相形见绌。这盏巨灯最终以惊人的方式坠毁，随之而来的大火焚毁了数栋建筑；但更严重的是，一尊朗洛依夫人的大型雕像在波及中被毁，导致她公开指控并惩处参与其中的官员与工匠，罪名是鼓动不敬、煽动叛乱。</p>\n<p>在这般不祥的事故之后，大多数制灯庆典又回归乡野，变得不那么引人注目。为呼应其作为军事工具的起源，如今村庄也会用制灯庆典进行基本通信。尽管络新妇对学术的清洗导致诸如信号技术之类的专门知识失传，但放飞天灯仍能传递简单却关键的信息。在首长夜，若地平线布满飞行的灯火，许多人会因此安心，知道邻里仍然活着，也仍能制作天灯。反之，如果附近的村庄在这个重要节日并未放飞自家的天灯，那么他们很可能已遭幽灵或更可怕之物所害。在这种情况下，邻近村落或会派出最勇敢的成员前去调查、提供协助，并在必要时为不得安息的亡者进行驱邪。</p>\n<p>一场制灯庆典包含两小时的设计与制作天灯，并在最后将所有天灯同时点燃放飞；比赛的胜者，是其天灯最能博得观众赞叹之人。在此期间，PC要进行4次制作检定以决定结果。</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>制灯庆典</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">专注</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">探索</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>技能检定</strong> @Check[crafting|dc:19]</p>\n</section>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Lantern Making\">Lantern Making</h2>\n<p>Sky Lanterns were originally developed during the days of Shu for sending military signals. The ancient practice now has a different cultural significance: representing reunions and people’s wishes finding their way to the heavens.</p>\n<p>These sky lanterns have bamboo frames and are made of oiled rice paper. They incorporate decor of fanciful shapes like birds (but never crows or ravens, due to the famed jorogumo’s intense dislike of tengu), dragons, and other flying creatures.</p>\n<p>For a while, spiders were extremely popular motifs for lantern design, perhaps as a sign of flattery to the jorogumo overlords; however, that trend fell out of favor several years ago. Officials wishing to curry Lady Lang Loi’s favor sponsored a grand lantern making ceremony and commissioned a massive lantern shaped like a golden orb-weaver spider, which was so large it eclipsed even the harvest moon. This gigantic lantern eventually crashed in spectacular fashion. The ensuing conflagration burned down several buildings, but the collateral destruction of a large statue of Lady Lang Loi resulted in her public accusations and punishments against the officials and artisans involved, on charges of encouraging disrespect and fomenting rebellion.</p>\n<p>After such an inauspicious incident, most lantern making ceremonies have resumed their rural, less high-profile focus. In a fitting nod to its functional military origins, villages now also use lantern making ceremonies for basic communications. While the jorogumo purge on scholarship has led to a loss of specialized knowledge like signaling techniques, it’s still possible to share simple yet vital information with released sky lanterns. On First Long Night, if the horizons are filled with flying lights, it reassures many to know their neighbors are still alive and able to construct sky lanterns. On the contrary, if a nearby village doesn’t release its own sky lanterns on this important festival, it’s very possible they’ve fallen to ghosts or worse. In such situations, neighboring villages might dispatch their bravest members to investigate, bring aid, and, if necessary, provide exorcisms for the unquiet dead.</p>\n<p>A lantern making ceremony involves two hours of designing and crafting lanterns, culminating in the lighting and release of them all at once; the winner of the contest is the one whose lantern evokes the most admiration from the spectators. The PC attempts four Crafting checks during this time to determine results.</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>Lantern Making Ceremony</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">Concentrate</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">Exploration</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>Skill Checks</strong> @Check[crafting|dc:19]</p>\n</section>"
            },
            "Bundle-Cutting": {
              "name": "割草绳 Bundle-Cutting",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Bundle-Cutting\">割草绳</h2>\n<p>在围观同伴的鼓励下，割草绳比赛的参与者努力将悬挂的稻草捆从紧密编织的格架上割落。比赛还有额外的变数：每位参与者在比赛开始时，手脚都会被麻绳松松地捆住。石匕首被放在远处的地面上，因此参与者必须先解开自己的束缚，然后冲刺去取回匕首，最后跑回来，在时限内尽可能多地割下稻草捆。</p>\n<p>割草绳源自训练练习，用以帮助村民应对被盗匪抓住并捆绑，或被林地陷阱困住的情况——这些技巧同样有助于从巨型蜘蛛的蛛网术以及其他类似生物的缚网中逃脱；这些危险早在络新妇统治之前就长期威胁着当地人。一旦参与者逃脱了自身束缚，接下来割断其他束缚便象征着迅速行动，拯救并释放被捆绑的盟友。</p>\n<p>当然，这类练习也能用于克服络新妇的蛛网术。为减少疑心并摆脱煽动叛乱的影射，绳索与绳结被设计得尽可能不像蛛网。比赛中高声唱出的农业歌曲，也同样令城里同谋者与络新妇占领者那挑剔的耳朵感到刺耳不适。因此，通过这场看似无害、伴随笑声、歌声与欢呼的比赛，神门的民众在众目睽睽之下磨练技能。也许有朝一日，这些技能不只用来割稻草，还能切开丝质蛛网与斑驳的甲壳。</p>\n<p>这项比赛的节奏比其他项目快得多；一场典型的割草绳对局会在1到2分钟内结束，取决于所选歌曲（无论时间长短，比赛仍通过四次技能检定结算）。</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>割草绳比赛</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">专注</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">探索</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>技能检定</strong> @Check[thievery|dc:17], @Check[athletics|dc:19], @Check[survival|dc:21]</p>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>特殊</strong> 在尝试任何技能检定之前，参与者必须先从自身的束缚中逃脱（DC 17）。若参与者在第一次尝试就逃脱，他们可以在不受减值影响的情况下尝试四次技能检定，以赢得割草绳比赛。若参与者需要进行两次逃脱尝试才能脱身，则其四次技能检定承受–1环境减值。若参与者需要进行三或四次逃脱尝试才能脱身，则其后续技能检定承受–2环境减值。第五次逃脱尝试必定成功，但在此之后，参与者的后续技能检定承受–3环境减值。</p>\n</section>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Bundle-Cutting\">Bundle-Cutting</h2>\n<p>Encouraged by onlooking fellows, participants in bundle-cutting contests strive to cut hanging bundles of straw loose from tightly-woven lattices. There’s an additional twist to this contest, as each participant begins the contest loosely bound hand and foot in twine ropes. Stone daggers are laid on the ground some distance away so that participants must first undo their own bindings, then dash to retrieve daggers, and finally run back to cut as many bundles free as they can within a time limit.</p>\n<p>Bundle-cutting evolved from training exercises to help villagers deal with being captured and bound by bandits or caught in woodland traps—techniques that are equally useful in escaping the webs of the giant spiders and similar creatures who have long posed dangers to the locals, even before jorogumo rule. Once a participant escapes their bindings, the cutting of other bindings represent swift action to rescue and release allies who have been bound.</p>\n<p>Such exercises, of course, have applications in overcoming the webbing of jorogumo as well. To reduce suspicions and shed implications of sedition, the ropes and knots are designed to look as unlike webs as possible. The loud farming songs sung during these games are also off-putting to the sophisticated eardrums of city-dwelling collaborators and jorogumo occupiers alike. Thus, through this innocuous contest punctuated by laughter, song, and cheering, Shenmen’s people sharpen their skills in plain sight. Perhaps these skills might be used one day not simply to slice straw, but also to cut through silken webs and mottled carapaces.</p>\n<p>This contest is much faster-paced than the others, with a typical bout of bundle-cutting resolving over the course of 1 to 2 minutes, depending on the song chosen (regardless of the time, the contest still resolves via four skill checks).</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>Bundle-Cutting Contest</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">Concentrate</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">Exploration</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>Skill Checks</strong> @Check[thievery|dc:17], @Check[athletics|dc:19], @Check[survival|dc:21]</p>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>Special</strong> Before any skill checks can be attempted, a participant must Escape (DC 17) from their bonds. If a participant Escapes on their first attempt, they can attempt the four skill checks to win bundle-cutting without penalty. If a participant requires two Escape attempts to get free, they take a –1 circumstance penalty to their four skill checks. If a participant requires three or four Escape attempts to get free, their following skill checks take a –2 circumstance penalty. A fifth Escape attempt is always successful, but after this, the participant takes a –3 circumstance penalty to their following skill checks.</p>\n</section>"
            },
            "Admiring the Moon": {
              "name": "咏月诗会 Admiring the Moon",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Admiring the Moon\">咏月诗会</h2>\n<p>咏月诗会是一种源自仪兴廷臣文化的游戏。面对中秋明月，参与者聚集在一起享用月饼与茶道，同时吟诗作赋，赞颂明月的优雅与美丽。精巧的文字游戏与意境深远的诗句会赢得喝彩与赞誉，而较为费力的比喻与警句则会引来鼓励与批评。随着夜色渐深，茶总会被酒与烈酒所取代，诗会也逐渐让位于关于形而上学与政治哲学的讨论。</p>\n<p>一场典型的咏月诗会会持续一个小时，期间包括饮食、社交与作诗。</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>咏月诗会</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">专注</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">探索</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>技能检定</strong> @Check[art-lore|dc:17], @Check[performance|dc:19], @Check[religion|dc:21]</p>\n</section>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Admiring the Moon\">Admiring the Moon</h2>\n<p>Admiring the Moon is a game with roots in Yixing courtier culture. Facing the harvest moon, participants gather to enjoy mooncakes and tea while composing poetry to extol the moon’s grace and beauty. Elegant wordplay and evocative verse win accolades and praises, while more labored metaphors and epigrams spur encouragements and critiques. As the night goes on, tea is invariably replaced with wine and spirits, and poetry gives way to discussions on metaphysics and political philosophy.</p>\n<p>A typical bout of Admiring the Moon involves an hour of eating, drinking, socializing, and composing of poems.</p>\n<section class=\"action\">\n  <h2 data-no-toc>Admiring the Moon</h2>\n\n  <ul class=\"traits\">\n    <li class=\"trait\">Concentrate</li>\n\n    <li class=\"trait\">Exploration</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"hanging-indent\"><strong>Skill Checks</strong> @Check[art-lore|dc:17], @Check[performance|dc:19], @Check[religion|dc:21]</p>\n</section>"
            },
            "Traditional Contests": {
              "name": "传统比赛 Traditional Contests",
              "text": "<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Traditional Contests\">传统比赛</h1>\n<p>以下三项比赛是首长夜庆典中传统的重头戏。每项赌博都通过在规定时间内进行4次技能检定来结算，以累计胜利点数（<em>Pathfinder GM Core</em> 184）。奖品可能从仅有的称赞与吹嘘资本，到真正的贵重物品或声望提升不等（赢得这些赌博可获得的奖品示例见@UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog07turnin.JournalEntryPage.07week3firstlo00]{第3周}）。在本冒险中，默认由单个PC参加比赛，且不与其他PC竞争，而是与村里的其他人较量。若PC选择参加其中一项比赛，他们可以进行4次技能检定来获得胜利点数——获得至少4点胜利点数的PC赢得比赛；获得至少6点胜利点数的PC则取得压倒性胜利，并可能获得额外奖励（示例见本冒险@UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog07turnin.JournalEntryPage.07week3firstlo00]{第3周}）。</p>\n<p>这些比赛中用于获取胜利点数的技能检定DC，假定参与者为4级；一方面因为首长夜发生在第5章期间，另一方面因为这个等级很适合作为典型小镇与村庄中最熟练的当地人既能感到有挑战、又能让本地专家拥有最佳获胜机会的水平。若你希望挑战不同等级的PC，可随意提高或降低这些DC。</p>\n<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Traditional Contests\">Traditional Contests</h1>\n<p>The following three contests serve as traditional highlights for First Long Night festivities. Each of these games are resolved via four skill checks attempted over the course of a set amount of time to accumulate Victory Points (<em>Pathfinder GM Core</em> 184). Prizes can vary from little more than accolades and bragging rights to actual valuables or increases to reputation (examples of prizes that can be earned by winning these games are presented on @UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog07turnin.JournalEntryPage.07week3firstlo00]{Week 3}). In this adventure, the assumption is that a single PC takes part in a contest and doesn’t compete against other PCs, instead competing against fellow villagers. A PC can attempt four skill checks to earn Victory Points if they choose to take part in one of these contests—a PC who earns at least 4 Victory Points wins the contest, while a PC who earns at least 6 Victory Points achieves an overwhelming win and might earn additional rewards (see @UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog07turnin.JournalEntryPage.07week3firstlo00]{Week 3} of this adventure for examples).</p>\n<p>The DCs of the skill checks to earn Victory Points for these contests assume that participants are 4th level, both because it’s during Chapter 5 that First Long Night takes place, and because this level is an appropriate one for the most skilled locals in typical small towns and villages to find challenges and to allow local experts the best chance to win. Feel free to increase or decrease these DCs if you wish to challenge PCs of different levels.</p>"
            },
            "Agility under Oppression": {
              "name": "压迫下的应变 Agility under Oppression",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Agility under Oppression\">压迫下的应变</h2>\n<p>将彩灯放上天空、割断绳索、吟诗赞月——这些根源或许看似无关紧要的零碎小事。然而，它们有双重目的：其一，让与世隔绝的村落能借由对“神门”身份的共同想象而凝聚；其二，使实用知识与技能得以在络新妇那傲慢的鼻子底下被秘密磨炼并保存下来。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Agility under Oppression\">Agility under Oppression</h2>\n<p>Releasing colorful lanterns into the sky, cutting ropes, poetry praising the moon—these traditions might appear irrelevant oddments. However, they fulfill twofold purposes: one, they allow isolated villages to bond over shared imaginations of a Shenmen identity; two, they facilitate surreptitious honing and preservation of practical knowledge and skills, right under the jorogumo’s haughty noses.</p>"
            },
            "Seasonal Markets": {
              "name": "应季市集 Seasonal Markets",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Seasonal Markets\">应季市集</h2>\n<p>村庄庆典的喧闹常常会吸引商人、祭司以及其他访客；当地人也乐于迅速接待这些旅人，并以食物、饮品与辛苦攒下的银粉来购买他们的货物与服务。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Seasonal Markets\">Seasonal Markets</h2>\n<p>The hubbub of a village’s celebrations often draws merchants, priests, and other visitors; locals are quick to host such travelers and offer to purchase their goods and services with food, drink, and hard-hoarded silver dust.</p>"
            },
            "Bribing Fumeiyoshi": {
              "name": "贿赂文业王 Bribing Fumeiyoshi",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Bribing Fumeiyoshi\">贿赂文业王</h2>\n<p>许多人会在坟茔附近撒下银粉来贿赂@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.deities.Item.6O4UGWcYERDEugzk]{文业王}，希望他会接受这些供品，从而不必再制造更多不死。贪婪的强盗可能会埋伏守候，抢劫村民或把散落的银粉据为己有；村民往往并不介意（只要他们能毫发无伤地逃脱），因为那样反而会把文业王的怒火引向这些贼人。</p>\n<img src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-dustedgrave.webp\" data-tooltip=\"Dusted Grave\" alt=\"Dusting a grave with silver dust\" class=\"float-right\" style=\"shape-outside:url('modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-dustedgrave.webp')\" />\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Bribing Fumeiyoshi\">Bribing Fumeiyoshi</h2>\n<p>Many scatter silver dust near grave sites to bribe @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.deities.Item.6O4UGWcYERDEugzk]{Fumeiyoshi}, hoping he would accept these offerings and not feel the need to create more undead. Greedy bandits might lurk in wait to rob villagers or pocket the strewn silver dust; villagers often don’t mind (providing they escape unharmed), as that simply draws Fumeiyoshi’s wrath upon the thieves instead.</p>"
            },
            "Ghosts... or Ancestors-": {
              "name": "幽灵......或祖先？ Ghosts... or Ancestors-",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Ghosts... or Ancestors?\">幽灵......或祖先？</h2>\n<p>神门的一些人对自己变成不死的恐惧，反而不如目睹挚爱之人经历这般可悲的异变来得强烈。无论如何，通过清理祖坟，他们阻止沉睡的亡者再度起身，化作暴戾的不死。有些人试图驱逐那些曾是家人的不死，从而将其净化后的魂魄送回家族，作为受人敬重的祖先被重新接纳。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Ghosts... or Ancestors?\">Ghosts... or Ancestors?</h2>\n<p>Some in Shenmen fear becoming undead less than witnessing loved ones undergoing such pitiable transformations. Regardless, by cleaning ancestral graves, they prevent the sleeping dead from arising as violent undead. Some seek to exorcise undead who were once family, and thus return their purified spirits to the fold as respected ancestors.</p>"
            },
            "Enlightened Self-Interest": {
              "name": "良性自利 Enlightened Self-Interest",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Enlightened Self-Interest\">良性自利</h2>\n<p>首长夜期间，许多人踏上危险的旅程去行善、清理老坟墓并举行临终仪式。以这种方式帮助他人——无论他们是活人还是死人——也同样是在帮助自己；毕竟，给邻居送食物或将其安葬要比处理因他们饥饿而死的尸体所化作的不死简单得多。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Enlightened Self-Interest\">Enlightened Self-Interest</h2>\n<p>First Long Night sees many embarking on dangerous journeys to do good deeds, clean old graves, and perform last rites. Helping others—be they living or dead—in this way also helps oneself; after all, it’s simpler to feed or bury neighbors rather than deal with undead arising from their starved corpses.</p>"
            },
            "Communal Revivals": {
              "name": "人际复兴 Communal Revivals",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Communal Revivals\">人际复兴</h2>\n<p>尽管残酷政权的历史撕裂了许多社会纽带，但首长夜是各个社群重新锻造这些联系的时代。从探望邻近村庄到接济隔壁人家，每一个小小的举动都让神门的社群再次更加紧密。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Communal Revivals\">Communal Revivals</h2>\n<p>While a history of brutal regimes have fractured many social bonds, First Long Night is a time when communities reforge these connections. From checking on neighboring villages to feeding the families next door, each small step brings Shenmen’s community closer again.</p>"
            },
            "Cold Nights, Warm Hearts": {
              "name": "冷夜暖心 Cold Nights, Warm Hearts",
              "text": "<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Cold Nights, Warm Hearts\">冷夜暖心</h2>\n<p>热甜品（如芝麻花生汤）与泡好的茶为众人共享，另有月饼、汤圆与水果。当地融合性的信仰鼓励这种慷慨之举，宣称这般温暖与团结必能打动来访的祖先或仁善的魂魄；它们或许会在未来的年份再度归来，并带来好运。</p>\n<h2 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Cold Nights, Warm Hearts\">Cold Nights, Warm Hearts</h2>\n<p>Hot desserts (such as sesame and peanut soup) and brewed tea are shared between all, along with mooncakes, glutinous rice balls, and fruits. Local syncretic beliefs encourage such generosity, stating such warmth and unity are sure to impress visiting ancestors or benevolent spirits who might then return in future years and bring good fortune with them.</p>"
            },
            "Festival Celebrations": {
              "name": "节日庆祝 Festival Celebrations",
              "text": "<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Celebrations\">节日庆祝</h1>\n<p>尽管其他天人可能会将首长夜描绘成一场严酷、毫无幽默感的守夜，用以对抗冬季与怪物，神门的居民却把它当作一场谨慎怀抱希望的节日来庆祝：在这一天，他们守护生命中那些微小的喜悦——这些喜悦若无人在意，便可能被冷酷的统治者榨取得一干二净，无论那些统治者是蛛形的、幽灵般的，还是其他什么。以下庆祝方式是在首长夜的诸多纪念活动中最为关键的几项。</p>\n<h1 data-no-toc data-split-here=\"Festival Celebrations\">Festival Celebrations</h1>\n<p>While other Tian might characterize First Long Night as a grim, humorless vigil against winter and monsters, Shenmen’s inhabitants celebrate it as a festival of cautious hope, where they protect life’s little joys that might otherwise be drained dry by uncaring overlords, be they arachnid, ghostly, or otherwise. The following celebrations are key among the ways in First Long Night is observed.</p>"
            },
            "First Long Night": {
              "name": "首长夜 First Long Night",
              "text": "<h1 class=\"chapter-heading no-toc\" data-split-here=\"First Long Night\">\n  <span>首长夜</span>\n</h1>\n\n<p>随着秋日渐渐让位于不断延长的夜晚，居住在神门乡间的人们开始为冬季做准备。这些准备围绕着乡村家庭对首长夜的庆祝来组织：那是一个共度的夜晚，人们以庄严的追思来纪念逝去的亲族，也见证社区对未来满怀希望的愿景。</p>\n<p>尽管首长夜与秋分相呼应，但节前活动提前一两周开始也并不罕见。这一做法源于当地对天枢宗教根源的改编；自仪兴皇朝以来，@UUID[Compendium.pf2e.deities.Item.QZD0u1jxwz0kj8uI]{法莱斯玛}与@UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog18sangpo]{善解}的信徒起初会在初秋择定多个日期，用以祭奠亡者并与自身的必死命运和解。</p>\n<aside class=\"sidebar float-right\">\n  <h1>引用在柳岸镇中...</h1>\n  <p>请注意，本文的大部分内容探讨的是首长夜这一节日在现代的存在方式。在“第二幕：任落叶飘零”期间，络新妇统治超过一个世纪的影响，并不会在节日根源如何发展这一点上成为同等重要的因素。本冒险包含建议，说明在那些不适用“缺少115年络新妇统治”这一前提的情况下，这些变化可能会如何影响部分庆祝活动与根源。</p>\n</aside>\n<p>首长夜是一场非常“身体力行”的盛事，甚至在节日到来之前便已如此。随着期待升温，当地人更起劲地劳作，希望提前完成各种职责，从而腾出时间为节日做准备；他们按着回响的民歌节拍干活，歌词与韵律里常常包含对传统农业技巧或本地传说的点滴洞见。秋日清冽的微风裹挟着柴火熏制的腌肉与熬炼油脂的香气，镇民们整理储备食物；而为临时节庆场地进行的施工声响，也一直持续到深夜。万事万物都鲜明、喧闹而有趣；在这座被幽灵盘桓的神门里，所有人都比平日更有几分精神抖擞。跳动的汗水与奔涌的鲜血、歌声、与季节更迭赛跑的忙碌……这一切都意在提醒生者与死者：生命有多么坚韧——无论是仍在继续的生命，还是只存在于魂魄记忆中的生命。</p>\n<p>尽管朗洛依及其党羽这支占领军自以为已牢牢扼住这座被蜘蛛诅咒的神门，首长夜依然呈现出一种静默的反抗：人们在此记起何为为自己而活，也记起那往往需要共同承担彼此尊重与求生的承诺。这些细小的颠覆会引向更大胆的举动；为了在没有官方支援的情况下熬过闹鬼的寒冬，哪怕最守法的居民也愿意违反络新妇占领者的律法，去搜索四处行走的驱魔人、强盗军火贩子与游荡的佣兵，以获取武器或解决办法，度过冷季中来自不死的威胁。尽管神门的络新妇通常会将这些宣示或积蓄权力的行为视为叛国，并以令人痛不欲生的死亡加以惩处，但出于唯有她们自己知晓的原因，她们一般不会在首长夜前后严格执行此类惩罚。</p>\n<img src=\"modules/pf2e-season-of-ghosts/assets/art/pzo90197-first-long-night.webp\" data-tooltip=\"First Long Night\" class=\"chapter-image\" />\n<h1 class=\"chapter-heading no-toc\" data-split-here=\"First Long Night\">\n  <span>First Long Night</span>\n</h1>\n\n<p>As autumn’s days yield to the burgeoning night, those who dwell in Shenmen’s countryside begin preparations for winter. These preparations are organized around rural families’ observation of First Long Night, an evening spent together in solemn commemorations of deceased kin as well as witnessing the community’s hopeful aspirations.</p>\n<p>While First Long Night speaks to the autumn equinox, it isn’t uncommon for pre-festivities to begin a fortnight or two before. Such a practice has its roots in local adaptations of Tian-Shu religious traditions; since the imperial Yixing, adherents of @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.deities.Item.QZD0u1jxwz0kj8uI]{Pharasma} and @UUID[JournalEntry.pf2apsog18sangpo]{Sangpotshi} originally set various dates in early autumn for paying respects to the dead and making peace with one’s mortality.</p>\n<aside class=\"sidebar float-right\">\n  <h1>In Willowshore...</h1>\n  <p>Note that much of this article explores how the festival of First Long Night exists in the modern day. During “Act 2: Let the Leaves Fall,” the impact of over a century of rule under the jorogumo isn’t as much a factor in how the festival’s traditions have grown. The adventure includes advice on how these changes might affect some of the celebrations and traditions where the lack of 115 years of jorogumo rule wouldn’t apply.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>First Long Night is a very physical event, even before the date of the festival arrives. As anticipation builds, locals work more exuberantly in hopes of getting ahead of duties so as to have time to prepare for the festival, working to the tempo of resonant folk songs with the lyrics and cadence often containing insights into traditional farming techniques or local legends. Fall’s crisp breeze carries the scent of wood-smoked preserved meat and rendered lard as townsfolk prepare food stores, while the noise of construction for temporary festival grounds goes on well into the evening. Everything is vivid, loud, and fun, and everyone is a little more spirited than usual in ghost-haunted Shenmen. The pulsing sweat and pouring blood, the songs, the race to beat the turn of seasons... all intended to remind both the living and dead of the tenacity of life—be it a life still being lived or one that exists only in a spirit’s memory.</p>\n<p>Even though the occupying forces of Lang Loi and her cronies think they have a stranglehold on spider-cursed Shenmen, First Long Night presents a quiet defiance, as people remember what it means to live for themselves and how often that requires shared commitments to mutual respect and survival. These tiny subversions lead to bolder ones; to survive haunted winters without official support, even the most law-abiding residents are willing to break the jorogumo occupiers’ laws to seek itinerant exorcists, bandit arms dealers, and wandering mercenaries to provide weapons or solutions to overcome the cold season’s undead threats. While the jorogumo of Shenmen normally consider these acts of asserting or accumulating power treasonous and punishable with excruciating death, for reasons known only to themselves, they don’t generally enforce such punishments near First Long Night.</p>"
            }
          }
        },
